Z||000004||terryk||04-17-2001||02:09 PM||||I have their black cradle bushings. I had to correct an error in the front bushings. Used a lathe to trim them down. I milled out a set of sleeves (to remove the rubber) and pressed the poly into the sleeves then installed them in the cradle. If you don't trim them, they don't seat correctly.<P>At least that's what I ran into. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-17-2001||07:34 PM||||I didn't have any problem getting the bushings in my cradel. In was easy. Getting the old ones Out, now that was a _____. Glad I only have to do that once...<P>I got mine well over a year ago... I wonder if someone mixed up somewhere in inventory or something.<P>After the miserable job cleaning out the old sleeves, I just squashed them in the shells with a big C clamp. Hardest part was getting clearance to use the C clamp under the car.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||04-17-2001||07:47 PM||||Getting the rubber out is super easy.<BR>Just run an approriately sized hole saw in from each side until the center sleeve and some rubber with it are cut loose. The rubber on the outer sleeve shoudl be pretty easy to remove after that. My rear control arm bushings weren't bonded to the outer sleeve or the inner sleeve.||||reg||1
